WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.980
Welcome to Top10Archive!

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.259
Savant syndrome is defined as a condition
in which a person demonstrates capacities

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:20.490
or abilities that are greatly in excess to
that of which would be considered normal.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.940
People with savant syndrome may have neurodevelopmental
disorders, notably autism spectrum disorders,

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.820
or brain injuries.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.160
For this installment, we're counting down
10 extraordinary savants that are capable

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.370
of mind-blowing things!

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.370
10.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.090
Jonathan Lerman
Jonathan Lerman is an autistic savant with

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.410
an IQ of just 53.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:47.000
At just 2-years old, Lerman would begin to
slide into long silences, and would be diagnosed

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.340
autistic, just a year later.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.270
While his autism made if difficult for him
to communicate in a typical manner, his artistic

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.590
ability would help him express what he was
thinking.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:02.329
By the age of 10, he began drawing charcoal
portraits of friends and people he had seen

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:05.680
- even if he had just made them up in his
mind.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.210
At the age of 14, Lerman had his pieces appearing
in New York art galleries.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.360
9.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.299
Stephen Wiltshire
Diagnosed with autism at a young age, Stephen

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:19.760
Wiltshire was sent to a school for special
needs children.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.890
There, his passions were encouraged and he
would find a love of drawing.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.780
Mute since a very young age, Stephen would
use his art to communicate since childhood

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.540
- not learning how to speak until he was 9.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:37.950
He began drawing highly detailed and incredibly
accurate portraits of things that he saw.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.370
Stephen's talent, however, goes much further
then just that.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:47.280
In 2009, he was flown over Tokyo for a mere
20 minutes and upon his return to the land,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.200
was able to draw a 10-meter or nearly 33-foot
long picture of the city.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.200
8.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.759
Daniel Tammet
Facing terrible epileptic seizures as well

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:03.130
as a diagnosis of autism at the age of four,
Daniel developed the ability to visualize

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.250
and calculate numbers in his head.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:13.810
Daniel Tammet can calculate numbers on a massive
scale, once reciting Pi from memory to the

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.060
22,514 decimal, a feat he accomplished on
Pi Day, to raise funds for the National Society

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.670
for Epilepsy.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:25.690
As well as his amazing mathematical talent,
Tammet is capable of learning languages at

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:30.819
an unprecedented rate - learning Icelandic
in just 7 days.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:36.060
Daniel's form of Autism is incredibly uncommon,
even among other savants, as he is able to

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.650
articulate his thoughts clearly.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.680
7.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.450
Kim Peek
If you've ever heard of the movie Rain Man,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.880
then you've heard the story of Kim Peek...
though you probably didn't know it.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:52.660
Born with severe brain damage, Kim's childhood
doctor suggested his father send him to an

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:57.400
institution and forget about him - as his
developmental disabilities were thought to

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.060
keep him from walking, let alone anything
else.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.569
Kim's father didn't agree, and returned home
with his son.

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:09.349
He does have problems walking and learning
is difficult for him - however, he has the

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:14.340
ability to read books two pages at once in
roughly 3 seconds.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.460
Not just that, but he remembers everything
he reads in them.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:19.460
6.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.390
Leslie Lemke
In an unfortunate twist of fate, Leslie Lemke

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:28.670
was born with severe birth defects, ones that
required the removal of his eyes.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:33.560
His birth mother gave him up for adoption,
and it was at that point that one, May Lemke,

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.110
took him in as one of her own at just 6 months
of age.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:43.220
At age 16, Leslie displayed his hidden talent
- when May woke up in the middle of the night

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:47.650
to find Leslie playing Tchaikovsky's Piano
Concerto No. 1.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.580
Leslie had no musical training, but still
managed to play flawlessly after just hearing

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.840
the song one time.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:00.020
Leslie now plays all kinds of music, and just
like he did with the Tchaikovsky piece, he

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.299
needs to only hear it once.

00:04:02.299 --> 00:04:03.480
5.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.610
Tony DeBlois
Born premature and underweight, Tony DeBlois

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.040
was hardly breathing.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.330
The doctors rushed to place an oxygen mask
on him, unbeknownst to them at the time, that

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.109
too much oxygen causes blindness.

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:22.650
Couple blindness with slow physical development,
then add in autism and you have the challenges

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.110
that Tony DeBlois faces every day.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.819
At age 2, Tony sat down in front of a piano
for the first time.

00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:35.250
Growing into music - he learned to play over
20 different instruments, including guitar,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.430
trumpet and the ukelele.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:42.960
It is estimated that Tony has roughly 8,000
musical pieces memorized which he learned

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.349
all by ear.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:45.349
4.

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:50.229
Jason Padgett
Though most people are born with savant syndrome,

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:55.089
others can acquire it through severe trauma
and brain damage - such as the case of Jason

00:04:55.089 --> 00:04:56.389
Padgett.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:01.029
After being brutally beaten during a mugging,
Jason awoke the next day in the hospital...

00:05:01.029 --> 00:05:03.240
though something was different.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.009
He now sees the world overlaid in complex
mathematical formulas.

00:05:08.009 --> 00:05:12.309
Over time he would begin to transcribe what
he saw through drawings.

00:05:12.309 --> 00:05:16.689
These drawings took the form of intricate
fractal form shapes and patterns.

00:05:16.689 --> 00:05:22.009
When neurologists took scans of his brain,
what they found was his brain activated rarely

00:05:22.009 --> 00:05:25.919
used portions to compensate for the damaged
areas.

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:26.919
3.

00:05:26.919 --> 00:05:31.500
Orlando Serrell
Orlando Serrell was not born autistic or brain

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.849
damaged, but rather known as an "acquired
savant".

00:05:34.849 --> 00:05:38.659
One day Serrell was playing baseball with
some friends at age 10.

00:05:38.659 --> 00:05:42.330
Serrell was struck in the left side of the
head by a ball, but was able to finish the

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:43.330
game.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:48.569
Figuring he was ok once his headache cleared,
he never thought to seek medical attention.

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:53.400
Serrell came to find he developed a talent
called "calendar calculating", meaning he

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.009
can tell you what day of the week any date
fell on, even more amazing as he can tell

00:05:58.009 --> 00:06:01.619
you what the weather was like on that day
where he was.

00:06:01.619 --> 00:06:02.619
2.

00:06:02.619 --> 00:06:06.080
Alonzo Clemons
Due to a head injury he suffered when he was

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.919
just a child, Alonzo Clemons lost much, but
in return gained a beautiful and magnificent

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:12.919
talent.

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:18.839
Determined by doctors to have an IQ of no
higher than 40, Clemons was left to be seemingly

00:06:18.839 --> 00:06:24.110
emotionless - though his face would light
up with joy with clay in his hands.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.610
You see, Clemons gained a gift of art.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:32.029
When he looks upon an animal, he is able to
create a sculpture of very fine detail using

00:06:32.029 --> 00:06:33.639
only his hands.

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:39.089
His sense of touch is so accurate that he's
even able to make these sculptures in the

00:06:39.089 --> 00:06:40.089
dark.

00:06:40.089 --> 00:06:41.089
1.

00:06:41.089 --> 00:06:45.289
Flo and Kay Lyman
Florence and Katherine Lyman, also known as

00:06:45.289 --> 00:06:51.580
the "Rainman Twins", are said to be the only
female autistic savant twins in the world.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.059
Even more impressive is the fact they are
the only pair of savant twins with the same

00:06:56.059 --> 00:06:57.159
talents.

00:06:57.159 --> 00:07:02.059
They have the ability of calendar calculation
and can tell you what the weather was like,

00:07:02.059 --> 00:07:08.050
what a TV personality was wearing and what
they ate for nearly every day of their lives.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.879
The twins even went as far as to document
what color clothing their favorite TV hosts

00:07:12.879 --> 00:07:52.270
wore, including Dick Clark from $100,000 pyramid.

